.TH WMLOCK 1 "2026" "Window Maker"
.SH NAME
wmlock \- X11 screen locker for Window Maker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmlock
[\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.br
.B wmlock
\fB\-\-help\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wmlock
locks every X screen on the current display behind a fullscreen cover
window showing a grayscaled, blurred snapshot of the desktop. The
keyboard and pointer are grabbed for the entire lifetime of the lock and
key events are forwarded to a WINGs password prompt that lives inside
the cover, so user input is not delivered to other clients while the
lock holds.
.PP
While there is no input the cover shows only a large centred clock and
date. Pressing a key, clicking, or moving the pointer reveals the
password prompt, and the keystroke that revealed it is delivered to the
field so the password can be typed straight away. Return and Escape are
the exception, on the clock view they only reveal the prompt. After
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
seconds without further input the prompt is hidden again, any partly
typed password is discarded, and the clock returns. The pointer is
hidden and the prompt itself stays keyboard only.
.PP
The lock releases when the invoking user's password is accepted.
Return submits, Backspace edits, Escape clears the field.
.PP
On builds with PAM, authentication uses the
.B wmlock
PAM service installed at
.IR /etc/pam.d/wmlock ,
which by default includes the
.B login
auth stack. Replace it to enable fingerprint or token modules without
touching the system login stack. On OpenBSD authentication uses
.BR auth_userokay (3)
with style
.BR auth-wmlock .
.PP
After
.B \-\-dpms\-timeout
seconds without input
.B wmlock
forces the monitor into DPMS Off and forces it back On at the next key,
button or motion event. The DPMS enabled or disabled state that was in
effect before the lock is restored when the prompt wakes and again on
exit, and the server's DPMS standby, suspend and off timeouts are never
changed, so an
.BR xset (1)
configuration outside the lock is preserved. Monitor blanking is
compiled out when the
.B xcb-dpms
development package was not present at build time and is silently
skipped at run time when the X server reports the display is not DPMS
capable.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-date\-format " FMT"
.BR strftime (3)
format for the date line. Default
.BR "%A %e %B" .
.TP
.BI \-\-dpms\-timeout " SECS"
Force the monitor into DPMS Off after
.I SECS
seconds without keyboard or pointer activity. A value of 0 disables
monitor blanking. Default 300.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit. The short
.B \-h
alias is also accepted.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle\-timeout " SECS"
Hide the prompt and show the clock after
.I SECS
seconds without keyboard or pointer activity. Default 10.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-idle\-clock
Keep the password prompt visible for the entire lock and never show the
clock. The lock then starts directly on the prompt.
.TP
.BI \-\-time\-format " FMT"
.BR strftime (3)
format for the large time line. The default follows the
.B LC_TIME
locale,
.B "%I:%M %p"
when the locale defines a 12-hour clock and
.B %H:%M
otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print best-effort diagnostics (OOM score, mlock, VT switch lock, PAM
failures) to standard error. The short
.B \-v
alias is also accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the Window Maker version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X display to lock and the value passed as
.B PAM_TTY
to PAM modules.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/wmlock
PAM service configuration used on PAM builds.
.SH NOTES
On Linux
.B wmlock
attempts to lower its OOM score, mlock the password buffer, and lock VT
switching. These are best effort and require additional capabilities
(typically the binary being installed setgid shadow or run from a
session with
.BR CAP_SYS_TTY_CONFIG ),
failure is reported only with
.BR \-\-verbose .
.SH SECURITY
.B wmlock
relies on X keyboard and pointer grabs and on an override-redirect
cover window, all of which are resources of an ordinary X client. Any
other client connected to the same X display can locate the cover via
.BR XQueryTree (3)
and issue
.BR XKillClient (3)
on it, which closes the
.B wmlock
connection, destroys the cover, and releases both grabs without
authentication. This is inherent to the X11 protocol and is shared by
every in-server X11 screen locker.
.PP
.B wmlock
therefore protects an unattended session only against access through
the physical keyboard and pointer. It does not place a security
boundary between X clients on the same display, and it should not be
relied on in environments where untrusted programs may hold an X
connection while the screen is locked.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
unlocked normally,
.B 1
could not establish the lock (display, grab, or PAM start failure),
.B 2
invalid command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmaker (1),
.BR xset (1),
.BR xss-lock (1)
